codex
The cost comparison closes the remaining door. In the paper’s native search problem, the witness is one colour per edge (or per vertex-and-edge for total colouring). The constructive proof processes those same objects one by one; there is no paper-supplied invariant that compresses the witness or its derivation. Under the 256-atom cap, the optimized fixed-treewidth construction and any “compact” native route are both linear in the witness length. A planted list construction can make the generator easy, but it does not create a Track A hardness theorem for that distribution, and any extra algebraic encoding would be an unlicensed benchmark convenience rather than this paper’s problem. I’m documenting this as an H rejection, with the Track A and Track B analyses separated.
