codex
I found one genuinely native Track B construction that survives the earlier objection, so I’m reopening the Step 0 decision before finalizing the rejection: use the paper’s bipartite list-edge-colouring theorem on \(K_{2,d}\), with every list an independently ordered cyclic translate of one hidden \(d\)-set. Any shared translate gives a proper colouring; generic construction is Galvin’s polynomial kernel/stable-matching method, while the compact route is one modular shift per edge. The plant and decoys are symmetric—there is no distinguished planted colour. I’m prototyping the adversary panel now; if ordinary greedy colouring succeeds, the rejection stands.
