codex
The prototype confirms the rejection. In the sparse/high-degree regime where a generic matching or Galvin construction would be costly enough to support Track B, ordinary greedy list colouring solved every tested instance. Tightening to a near-square \(K_{16,16}\) made greedy fail, but then the shared-translate/Latin-square ansatz itself is immediate and solves every instance in one operation per output edge; it is exactly the required fourth in-context attack. The answer also rises to 252–256 atoms. I’m adding those measured triage results to the rejection record.
